From this year's latest exam results we can see languages at the top end of the tables with the highest rates among private schools.
Altoge-filled 68 per per cent of pupils who studied foreign languages received a higher score of A.
The figures, collected from a total of 74 featuring SCIS schools, show 72 per per cent of pupils in Mandarin received the same graduation, 72 per per per cent of German learners, 69 per per per cent of those who have Spanish learners.
It shows that private schools in Scotland support foreign languages as essential for their lives that children and young people undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages, which are now part of the choices of the subjects, are included in private schools and other school syllacators in the same way as STEM subjects (science, tech, engineering and maths).
A 2014 study by the UK's Committee on Work and Skills (UK Commission for Employment and Skills) has found that the reasons why employers find it difficult to find job vacancies highlighted a lack of language skills.
As a result, language skills are becoming more and more of a requirement to prepare young people for their future work.
To the extent that more expected job offerings require language skills, such a skill is becoming essential in a globalised world.
Despite the career choices made, acquiring a different language will give a person a real benefit in the future, as language skills will be life-changing.
The ability to communicate directly with foreign citizens instantly gives multilingual people a competitive advantage.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75 per cent of respondents don't speak a foreign language well enough to have a two-dollar sample; the only language with a double-digit percentage was French at 15 per cent.
That's why it's now important for children in language learning.
Competance of a number of languages, especially those of emerging economies, will give children a better chance of finding a good job.
Various languages are taught in every school in Scotland.
Several schools will instead focus on classical modern languages, whereas in other schools they will be taught the languages, which are considered the most important, such as mandarin or Japanese in the UK.
Despite the interests of the child, there is always an opportunity in private schools to choose between a wide range of languages taught by experts in their field.
Scottish private schools are committed to providing a learning environment that prepares children and equips them with the skills they need to achieve success, no matter what the future holds.
It can no longer be in denial that, in a globalised business environment, languages are crucial for the country's future, which is why this must also be reflected in education.
